Keep a character split across iconv input blocks when ignoring errors.
With //IGNORE, as iconv -c adds, iconv() treated every failure from the
input decoder as an invalid byte to skip, including EINVAL, which means
only that the buffer ends inside a character. process_file() reads the
input in blocks of BUFSIZ bytes, so a multibyte character that fell
across a block boundary lost its first part as "invalid", and its
remaining bytes were then discarded too at the start of the next block:
iconv -c -f UTF-8 silently dropped such characters, and with UTF-16 a
surrogate pair across a boundary shifted the rest of the input by a
byte, garbling it. Return EINVAL to the caller even with //IGNORE, as
glibc does, so that process_file() carries the partial character over
to the next block. Input that really ends inside a character now fails
with "Invalid argument" under -c too, as with GNU iconv, and so does
input in a code page that mbcs_mblen() cannot measure, such as UTF-7,
instead of being discarded entirely with status 0.

---
 miscutils/iconv.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/miscutils/iconv.c
+++ b/miscutils/iconv.c
@@ -724,7 +724,7 @@
 		insize = cd->from.mbtowc(&cd->from, (const uchar *)*inbuf, *inbytesleft, wbuf, &wsize);
 		if (insize == -1)
 		{
-			if (cd->to.flags & FLAG_IGNORE)
+			if ((cd->to.flags & FLAG_IGNORE) && errno != EINVAL)
 			{
 				cd->from.mode = frommode;
 				insize = 1;
